<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grenada</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Island of Spice</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grenada is an archipelagic country of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the southernmost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a>, made up of Grenada itself, Carriacou and Petite Martinique. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> people lived there when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> sighted it in 1498, and their resistance defeated early European attempts at colonisation until French settlement began in 1649. Between 1669 and 1808 approximately 127,600 African slaves were brought to the island by the British and French. It was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1763 and gained independence on 7 February 1974 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-gairy">Eric Gairy</a>. A coup in March 1979 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-bishop">Maurice Bishop</a> to power, and a U.S.-led invasion followed his execution in October 1983.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It spread across the eastern Caribbean in the 1970s, and Rastas took part in <b>Grenada</b>'s New Jewel Movement of 1979.</blockquote><a href="/religion/rastafari">Rastafari · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeology in <b>Grenada</b> and Saint Vincent supports integration.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
